      "mistake": "Do not write that DMA means 'no interrupt at all'. DMA raises <strong>exactly one</strong> interrupt, at the end of the whole transfer. The saving is in the <em>number</em> of interrupts and in the CPU not copying the bytes — not in eliminating interrupts.",
      "examAnswer": "Input and output devices are connected to a computer through device controllers, which are electronic components containing control, status and data registers together with a local buffer, and which present a uniform register interface to the processor. The processor may perform input and output by programmed input/output, in which it repeatedly polls the status register and transfers each byte itself, or by interrupt-driven input/output, in which the controller raises an interrupt when it is ready, thereby freeing the processor from polling. Both methods nevertheless require the processor to transfer every byte. Direct Memory Access overcomes this by employing a DMA controller that is given the memory address, the transfer count and the direction, and which then transfers an entire block directly between the device and main memory without processor involvement, raising a single interrupt only when the transfer is complete. Because the DMA controller and the processor contend for the memory bus, DMA transfers briefly delay the processor, a phenomenon known as cycle stealing.",
      "remember": "Polling = CPU asks repeatedly. Interrupt = device tells CPU. DMA = device writes to memory itself, one interrupt at the end.",
      "why": "DMA is why you can copy a 2 GB file and still scroll smoothly. It is also one of the most common five-mark questions in the I/O section.",
      "practice": "A 4 KB disk block is transferred using interrupt-driven I/O with one interrupt per word (4 bytes), and then using DMA. How many interrupts occur in each case?"
