      "examAnswer": "Input/output software is structured in four layers, each of which hides detail from the layer above it. The lowest layer consists of interrupt handlers, which respond to the completion interrupt raised by a device controller and unblock the driver that was waiting for it; interrupts are deliberately confined to this layer so that the remainder of the system need not be aware of them. Above this lie the device drivers, which are the only components that know the specific registers and commands of a particular device and which translate abstract requests into the corresponding hardware operations. The third layer is the device-independent operating system software, which performs the functions common to all devices, namely uniform interfacing to drivers, device naming and protection, provision of a device-independent block size, buffering, allocation and release of dedicated devices, and error reporting. The highest layer is user-level software, comprising library procedures such as printf that are linked into user programs, together with spooling systems in which a daemon process manages a dedicated device such as a printer. The principal goals of this design are device independence, uniform naming, error handling at the lowest possible level, and presenting inherently asynchronous transfers to user programs as simple synchronous operations.",
