    {
      "title": "Disk Hardware and Access Time",
      "teacher": "Reading from RAM takes about 100 nanoseconds. Reading from a hard disk takes about 10 milliseconds — that is one hundred thousand times slower. Why? Because something physical has to move. Let's look inside the disk and find out exactly what is moving, and how long each movement takes.",
      "explain": "A hard disk has rotating <strong>platters</strong> divided into circular <strong>tracks</strong>, which are divided into <strong>sectors</strong>. The same track number on all platters forms a <strong>cylinder</strong>. Access time is the sum of <strong>seek time</strong>, <strong>rotational latency</strong> and <strong>transfer time</strong>.",
      "keyPoints": [
