        },
        {
          "title": "Where the time actually goes",
          "text": "<strong>Seek time</strong> is mechanical arm movement and is typically 5 to 10 milliseconds — by far the biggest component and the only one software can influence. <strong>Rotational latency</strong> depends only on the spin speed: on average you wait half a rotation, so at 7200 RPM (one rotation = 60000/7200 = 8.33 ms) the average latency is about 4.17 ms. <strong>Transfer time</strong> is usually well under a millisecond for one sector. This is the whole reason disk scheduling exists: attack the biggest number."
        },
        {
          "title": "Why the disk is the bottleneck",
          "text": "A CPU instruction takes under a nanosecond; a RAM access takes about 100 nanoseconds; a disk access takes about 10 milliseconds. In the time one disk read completes, the CPU could have executed roughly ten million instructions. Every trick in this unit — caching, buffering, scheduling, RAM disks — exists to avoid or shorten that wait."
