      "realLife": "Think of an old jukebox or a record player. Moving the arm across to the right song is the <strong>seek</strong>. Waiting for the record to spin around to the start of that song is the <strong>rotational latency</strong>. Actually playing the song is the <strong>transfer</strong>. Moving the arm is the slow, clumsy part — exactly as in a disk.",
      "worked": "<strong>Calculating disk access time.</strong> A disk spins at <strong>6000 RPM</strong>, has an average seek time of <strong>6 ms</strong>, and a transfer rate of <strong>50 MB per second</strong>. Find the average time to read one 4 KB block.<br><br><strong>Step 1 — Seek time</strong> = 6 ms (given).<br><br><strong>Step 2 — Rotational latency.</strong> One full rotation takes 60 / 6000 seconds = 0.01 s = <strong>10 ms</strong>. On average we wait half a rotation, so latency = 10 / 2 = <strong>5 ms</strong>.<br>(Shortcut: average latency in ms = 30000 / RPM = 30000 / 6000 = 5 ms.)<br><br><strong>Step 3 — Transfer time.</strong> 4 KB = 4096 bytes. At 50 MB/s = 50 × 1024 × 1024 bytes/s, time = 4096 / 52428800 s ≈ 0.000078 s ≈ <strong>0.08 ms</strong>.<br><br><strong>Step 4 — Total access time</strong> = 6 + 5 + 0.08 = <strong>11.08 ms</strong>.<br><br>Look at the split: seek 6 ms, latency 5 ms, transfer only 0.08 ms. Over <strong>99 per cent</strong> of the time is spent just getting to the data. That single observation is the justification for every disk scheduling algorithm in the next topic.",
      "table": {
