      "examAnswer": "A magnetic disk consists of one or more rotating platters, each surface of which is divided into concentric circular tracks that are further divided into sectors, the sector being the smallest addressable unit. The set of tracks occupying the same relative position on all surfaces constitutes a cylinder, which can be accessed without any movement of the read/write arm. The total time required to access a block of data is the sum of three components: the seek time, which is the time taken by the arm to position itself over the required cylinder; the rotational latency, which is the time taken for the required sector to rotate beneath the head and which averages one half of a rotation; and the transfer time, which is the time actually required to read or write the data. Since seek time is normally the dominant component, disk scheduling algorithms attempt to minimise the total movement of the read/write head.",
