    {
      "title": "Disk Scheduling Algorithms",
      "teacher": "Eight people in your building have pressed the lift button, on floors all over the place. Should the lift serve them in the exact order they pressed? Or should it just sweep up and then sweep down? That choice is worth real seconds — and inside a computer it is worth a huge amount of performance. Let's do the numbers.",
      "explain": "Several disk requests usually wait in a queue at once. <strong>Disk scheduling</strong> decides the order in which to serve them so that the total <strong>head movement</strong> (and therefore the seek time) is as small as possible. The six standard algorithms are <strong>FCFS, SSTF, SCAN, C-SCAN, LOOK</strong> and <strong>C-LOOK</strong>.",
      "keyPoints": [
