      "mistake": "Three classic errors. <strong>(1)</strong> Forgetting to count the 199-to-0 jump in C-SCAN — it <em>is</em> counted as head movement (199 cylinders here). <strong>(2)</strong> Going all the way to 199 in LOOK — LOOK stops at 183. <strong>(3)</strong> Not stating the direction of travel. SCAN with the head moving <em>downward</em> gives a completely different total, so always write 'assuming the head moves towards higher cylinders'.",
      "examAnswer": "Disk scheduling determines the order in which pending disk requests are serviced, with the objective of minimising the total movement of the read/write head and hence the seek time. In first-come first-served scheduling requests are served in arrival order, which is fair but may cause large and wasteful head movements. Shortest seek time first always selects the pending request closest to the current head position, which substantially reduces total movement but may indefinitely postpone requests that lie far from the head. The SCAN or elevator algorithm moves the head in one direction, servicing all requests encountered until it reaches the end of the disk, and then reverses direction. C-SCAN treats the cylinders as a circular list, servicing requests in one direction only and returning immediately to the opposite end without servicing, which produces a more uniform waiting time. LOOK and C-LOOK are refinements of SCAN and C-SCAN respectively in which the head reverses or returns at the last request in the current direction rather than at the physical end of the disk, thereby avoiding unnecessary travel. For the request queue 98, 183, 37, 122, 14, 124, 65, 67 with the head initially at cylinder 53 and moving towards higher cylinder numbers on a disk of 200 cylinders, the total head movements are 640, 236, 331, 382, 299 and 322 cylinders for FCFS, SSTF, SCAN, C-SCAN, LOOK and C-LOOK respectively.",
