    {
      "title": "Disk Error Handling, Caching, RAM Disks and Clocks",
      "teacher": "One last set of ideas, and they all share one theme: the disk is slow and imperfect, so the OS quietly patches around it. And then there is the clock — the tiniest piece of hardware in the machine, doing the single most important job of all: telling the OS when to stop one process and start another.",
      "explain": "Disks come from the factory with <strong>bad sectors</strong>, so the controller hides them. <strong>Track-at-a-time caching</strong> and <strong>RAM disks</strong> both trade memory for speed. And the <strong>clock</strong> (timer) is the hardware that makes preemptive multitasking possible at all.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "Every disk leaves the factory with some <strong>bad sectors</strong> — this is normal and expected.",
        "<strong>Sector sparing (forwarding)</strong>: the controller substitutes a spare sector for a bad one, invisibly.",
